From 54c9352521357b72ec0a6bed732f778733f46a9a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Schaka <jonastanzer@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 11 Aug 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 3/3] rocr: defer GPU virtual-address reuse for gfx803 (Polaris)

libhsakmt's fmm aperture allocator recycles a freed GPU virtual address
(VA) immediately, handing it straight back to the very next allocation of
a compatible size. On gfx803 this corrupts MIOpen CK-style reduction
kernels: a kernel still in flight that reads a buffer freed behind its
back can land on a VA already reassigned to different content. Confirmed
via this repo's own 52-shape reduce-harness sweep: unpatched ~10/52 fail
every run (30/30 runs), patched 0/52 across 100/100 runs.

Fix: park freed objects alive (BOs kept, GPU mapping and content intact,
VA reserved) on a length-bounded per-aperture FIFO
(FMM_REUSE_QUEUE_MAX=256 entries / FMM_REUSE_BYTES_MAX=1GiB), and only
really free them inside a LATER free call once the window expires --
while the GPU pipeline is typically busy, which is the measured
ingredient that clears the stale gfx803 memory-system state (idle-time
teardown at a quiescent point does not). Scratch apertures bypass the
FIFO entirely, since fmm_release_scratch's teardown loop requires
immediate removal. Also defaults HSA_DISABLE_FRAGMENT_ALLOCATOR to ON,
since hsa-runtime's SimpleHeap fragment sub-allocator is a second,
higher-layer VA recycler the fmm-level fix cannot see.

Not carried in lucbruni-amd's TheRock branch -- this repo's own addition,
originally shipped against rocm-6.4.4 and re-diffed here for rocm-systems
commit 2b22ab0195 (therock-7.14). See
patches/rocm-systems/va-reuse-defer.patch in ROCm-gfx803 for the full
root-cause investigation, rejected non-solutions, and hardware validation
numbers this commit message only summarizes.

NOT YET RE-VERIFIED under TheRock's own build against real gfx803
hardware -- re-diffed and read against the current source, not re-run
through the reduce-harness on binaries this pipeline itself produced.
---
